  "prompt_text": "The figure presents ammonia as a bridge between renewable electricity and end use. On the left, several renewable sources—solar, wind, hydropower, and ocean wave power—feed two key upstream steps: air separation to obtain nitrogen and water electrolysis to produce hydrogen. These streams are then combined in the center to make NH3.\n\nThe middle section emphasizes both practical production and catalyst science. A schematic surface-reaction model shows nitrogen and hydrogen species converting on a catalyst under proton/electron input, while a volcano plot suggests a theoretical relationship between catalytic activity and adsorption energy. Below, catalyst synthesis and characterization are linked to improving NH3 synthesis, which is depicted as a reactor stack producing ammonia.\n\nOnce formed, ammonia can be stored and transported, shown by tank farms and a ship, highlighting its role as an energy carrier and chemical commodity. The right side shows major utilization routes: direct heat and power generation, vehicle and marine fuels, decomposition of NH3, and use in ammonia fuel cells. Overall, the figure summarizes a full renewable-ammonia value chain, from clean power and feedstock preparation through catalytic synthesis, storage, transport, and multiple energy applications.",
